  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how therapeutic antibodies work in the bloodstream and why humanized antibodies can trigger unwanted immune responses. On the left, it contrasts a fully human antibody with a humanized antibody, which is mostly human but still contains small murine-derived regions.\n\nIn the upper bloodstream scene, a therapeutic antibody binds the circulating target PCSK9, including PCSK9 associated with LDL particles, showing the intended drug action of neutralizing the target in blood. Red blood cells and white blood cells provide the vascular context, while the antibody–antigen complexes remain soluble.\n\nThe lower scene shows the same general setting but adds a second class of antibodies, labeled neutralizing antibodies, directed against the therapeutic antibody itself. These anti-drug antibodies recognize exposed nonhuman regions on the humanized therapeutic antibody and bind to it, sometimes while it is also bound to PCSK9. This visually conveys immunogenicity: the patient’s immune system can mount antibodies against the therapy, potentially blocking its interaction with PCSK9 or forming larger immune complexes.\n\nOverall, the main message is that antibody drugs can effectively neutralize circulating targets such as PCSK9, but nonhuman antibody elements may provoke neutralizing anti-drug antibodies that reduce treatment efficacy.",
